Ministry of Pest Control
Phnom Penh: The Ministry of Agriculture has started to disseminate biological measures to control pests and diseases to farmers in order to reduce the use of chemical pesticides that will contribute to reducing farmers' costs as well as increase agricultural yields.
Biological measures to control pests and diseases, which were discussed yesterday at a seminar on the use and commercialization of biological measures, is the use of a fungus called Trichoderma.
Kien Sophea, deputy director of the Department of Horticulture, Experiments and Combined Crops, which has been studying Trichoderma since 2007, said the biological measure was to get rid of pests.
The measure has been successfully tested with more than 100 farming families for three years in five provinces across Cambodia.
He explained:
He said that the trichoderma has the benefit of reducing the use of pesticides, secondly, increase yields and thirdly help the environment better and more economical than the use of pesticides because it has the ability to help. Crops grow well and reduce pest damage.
According to Kien Sophea, this trichoderma mushroom originates in the soil.
It is a fungus that is a small organ that can be seen under a microscope through a laboratory and is made into a trichoderma powder.
One kilogram of tricoderma powder costs as little as $ 8 and can be mixed with 1,000 liters of water to irrigate crops, prevent pest infestation, as well as compost to nourish plant roots to grow well and yield high yields. .
Most farmers face pests and diseases that require them to pay for pesticides and expensive chemical fertilizers.
Nim Kuon, a rice farmer in Battambang province, said his rice crop was threatened by pests and insects, which required him to spend 200,000 riel on a single hectare of pesticide spray to get rid of it. That pest.
In a season, if there are a lot of insects, he has to spend three to four times to spray pesticides.
He will return to using biological measures if the promotion reaches his location.
If there are experts to visit and show the effectiveness directly.
Him Im, a pepper farmer in Kampong Cham province, said he spent about $ 300 a season on pesticides alone.
He said that most pepper farmers are now facing a fungal disease that eats the roots of pepper trees, which are causing damage to hundreds of pepper plants.
Farmers pay $ 20 to grow one pepper or one plant.
Keam Makarady, director of CEDAC's Environment and Health Program, said at the workshop that agricultural practices using natural measures increase the crop yields of hundreds of farmers.
Mr. So Khon Rithykun, Director General of the Department of Agriculture of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, said:
He said the ministry had generally stepped up work to protect the crop by reducing the use of chemicals, both chemical fertilizers and pesticides, until finally there was a law on the use of pesticides.
Banteay Meanchey Provincial Department Opens Agricultural Technical Zone
Banteay Meanchey: Banteay Meanchey provincial authorities on Wednesday officially inaugurated a model park for the development of agricultural technology, the first large-scale agricultural skills training venue in the province.
The agricultural technical zone, built at a cost of nearly $ 1 million, covers a total area of 10 hectares, is an agricultural skills development project funded by the Yunnan Provincial Department of Agriculture.
Banteay Meanchey provincial governor Ko Sum Saroeut and Xiao Yi, director of the International Relations Office of the Yunnan Provincial Department of Agriculture, presided over the inauguration.
Mr. Saroeut told the Post yesterday that the presence of the Agricultural Science and Technology Zone would improve agricultural production in Banteay Meanchey, as the province previously had only small-scale agricultural training schools. Only one.
Through the new agricultural technical zone, the Yunnan Provincial Department of Agriculture provided four Chinese trainers to train farmers and also sent agricultural experts to study agricultural skills in Yunnan.
Cambodia spends $ 442 million on oil
Cambodia spent more than $ 442 million on fuel imports in the first quarter of this year, up 11 per cent from the same period last year.
According to data from the first quarter of 2013, Cambodia spent $ 397 million on fuel imports.
Experts say the increase is reflecting an increase in domestic production.
Data from the Ministry of Commerce, obtained by the Post yesterday, showed that Cambodia imported 453,000 tonnes of fuel in the first quarter of 2014, up 10 per cent from 412,190 tonnes in the same period last year. Each other from last year.
Independent economist Srey Chanthy said increased economic activity and increased domestic consumption for production and transportation were important components for Cambodia to import more oil.
Currently, Cambodia spends more than $ 1 billion to import more than 1 million tonnes of fuel, mostly from Vietnam, Singapore and Thailand.
Although US-based Chevron has been licensed by the Cambodian government to explore for oil in Block A, extraction has been postponed to 2019.
The Ministry of Commerce's daily commodity data shows that the price of oil in Cambodia is 5,200 riel and 5,400 riel per liter.
Cambodia also plans to build a $ 2.3 billion refinery on 80 hectares of land on the border of Sihanoukville and Kampot provinces.
The Post recently reported that construction of the plant had been delayed again due to delays in planning.
