He said that the future of the Cambodian economy and the well-being of the younger generation depend on the creation of a young skilled workforce that can adapt and compete regionally and globally.
Japanese investment in Cambodia soars
Cambodia received $ 23 million in direct investment from Japan in the first three months of this year, equivalent to half of Japan's investment in Cambodia in 2013.
This is according to data from the Japanese Embassy in Cambodia.
An economist at the Japanese Embassy in Cambodia told the Post that direct investment flows from Japan to Cambodia would continue to grow at a good rate this year, despite the country's political instability.
Tayoshi Kiromiya, a senior adviser at the Japanese Embassy in Cambodia, said that the Japanese investment projects that came in earlier this year included investments in factories for the construction of houses, factories made of Animal skins and animal feed factories.
It may indicate that Japanese investment will continue to grow strongly in Cambodia for this year as well.
He said Cambodia was competitive in terms of large and low-cost labor, while labor costs in China, Thailand and many other major Japanese investment destinations were rising.
Direct investment flows from Japan to Cambodia have increased significantly in recent years, including investment projects in factories, restaurants, hotels and supermarkets.
Japan's Aeon Mall project, which will be the first international mall in Cambodia, will officially open on June 30, with a number of well-known retailers.
Local and international vendors have already signed up to operate a total of 186 companies in the market.
According to investment data from the Council for the Development of Cambodia (CDC), from 1994 to 2013, Japanese investment in Cambodia totaled $ 593 million, of which more than half of the total investment in 2012 alone.
