Cambodia to consider tax reform on mining industry
The Cambodian government will consider a number of changes to Cambodia's mining laws, including a rethink of high tax rates in the sector.
Citing talks with representatives of the Western Australian mining industry last week, Meng Saktheara, secretary of state at the Ministry of Mines and Energy, said Cambodia's existing 30 per cent tax on mining and oil and gas exploration operations was Outdated.
He added that the ministry had not yet discussed with the Ministry of Commerce regarding the proposed change.
Meng Saktheara's comments came days after officials from the Ministry of Mines and Energy returned from a week-long visit to Western Australia's mining industry.
The visit was organized in part by the Western Australian Government's International Center for Development Development.
Last week, the Australian coalition government formally waived the controversial MRRT tax levied by the previous government in 2012.
Mr. Theara said the Cambodian government would consider raising the lease tax on mining only for oil and gas companies.
The current rental tax on Australia's oil, gas and oil exploration operations is currently 40 per cent.
According to the ministry, a complete law on oil and gas extraction and new mining licensing regulations will be drafted by the end of 2015, with laws related to workers' safety, social responsibility and biodiversity also being strengthened. Well.
Mam Sambath, executive director of DPA-EISEI, a mining industry watchdog, welcomed Mr. Theara's remarks cautiously, calling for further consultation between the community and the ministry regarding mining reform.
