Cassava prices rise slightly as yields fall
Pailin: The price of cassava in Pailin province during the harvest season has risen slightly as yields have fallen due to weather conditions.
This is according to an official from the Pailin Provincial Department of Agriculture.
Chen Chin, deputy director of the Pailin provincial agriculture department, said yesterday that heavy rains and floods from September to October 2013 had reduced cassava yields this season.
He added that the slight increase in prices was due to an increase in local buyers, stockpiles for export and two large cassava collectors just present in Pailin, which reduced farmers' reliance on Thai market.
Most Pailin farmers have switched from corn to cassava.
Figures from the Pailin Provincial Department of Agriculture show that the total cultivated area in the province is 37,000 hectares, of which 80 percent is cassava.
On average, farmers get 30 to 40 tons of cassava per hectare.
Meas Mol, a farmer who grows cassava on 50 hectares of land, said that five years ago the price of fresh cassava fell to 120 riel due to declining market demand in Thailand, forcing farmers to sell their produce even if it was not. Profits also because of the need for working capital.
He hopes prices will remain stable for next year's harvest.
He said good prices during the harvest season had recovered from losses.
The cassava harvest season in Pailin province runs from December to April.
Fresh and dried cassava is sold to local collectors, who will later export it to Thailand, Malaysia and China.
Moul Sam, an accountant at Chamroeun Chey, said his company bought 20,000 to 60,000 tonnes of cassava from farmers.
He welcomed the presence of Malaysian and Chinese collectors, although it added to the competition.
Yields this year are down, but prices are good.
This is a good thing for farmers.
All kinds of tax payment service can be done through ACLEDA
Phnom Penh: ACLEDA Bank has expanded its licensing services to the public and businesses that are required to pay taxes to the state.
From now on, all types of tax payments, citizens and traders can have the option to pay taxes through ACLEDA Bank or directly to government institutions after the Ministry of Economy and Finance has allowed the tax payment process through this bank to facilitate and Strengthen transparency.
The tax service through ACLEDA Bank was initially limited to taxes on means of transportation such as motorcycles.
ACLEDA Bank yesterday announced the launch of all types of tax collection services for the public who are obliged to pay taxes to the state, who can pay taxes at all 251 ACLEDA Bank branches nationwide without having to go directly to the General Department. Customs and Excise or the General Department of Taxation as before.
In the past, customers could also pay taxes through ACLEDA Bank, but at that time only vehicles were taxed.
In Channy, CEO of ACLEDA Bank, confirmed to the Post yesterday that the bank's tax payment service will improve the quality of public services and allow the public to pay all taxes owed to them. Obligations without having to pay the service fee to the bank.
He said it would also help the financial sector, which would allow more money to circulate in financial markets for economic growth.
Taxes to the state are made in riel, but in the event that the customer pays in a different currency, the bank will make the payment based on the actual exchange rate.
According to tax revenue figures from the General Department of Taxation, the Cambodian government collected a total of $ 881 million in tax revenue in 2013.
In the first three months of this year, Cambodia collected $ 192 million in taxes.
Mey Van, director of the Department of Financial Industry of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, said the decision to allow the payment of taxes through banking services was part of the Royal Government of Cambodia's reforms to increase transparency and efficiency in fundraising. Tax revenue for the national budget.
He added that it is a good measure to facilitate the business process for traders and also to reduce the hassle of verifying documents in the past.
Clint OConnell, Phnom Penh-based commercial legal adviser for VDB, welcomed the new procedure, saying the way businesses would be made more competitive after tax payments could be made through ACLEDA Bank because it has branches almost everywhere in the country.
By doing so, it also increases the government's tax revenue.
Cambodia should continue its efforts to reform its economy to boost growth
Asia is in a good position to capture a significant amount of capital inflows from the global best trends with steady growth in 2014-2015.
With more vigilance and more reforms in place, the region should maintain a recovery from global risks and continue to be a powerful force in promoting empowerment. The recovery.
With Cambodia's regional integration, this is good news and provides more opportunities to focus on policies to further boost the economy from the impact.
How do we reach these conclusions in our latest regional economic outlook report?
First, global action has been strengthened, and this will help Asia's exports.
Growth momentum has concentrated in the United States and the eurozone as easing of fiscal tightening and favorable monetary conditions remain.
As some of the major emerging market economies slowed, overall growth in emerging markets increased.
Second, the region's leading economies are also doing well, despite some challenges.
In China, the introduction of the government's reform agenda boosted confidence, with average growth slightly higher to 7.5 percent in 2014.
Meanwhile, Abe's economic plan boosted confidence and inflation in Japan, and growth should keep the trend above 1.4 percent in 2014.
This favorable foreign environment is benefiting other countries in the region, along with strong job growth and credit in many economies.
The overall outlook for Asia is part of a strong stabilization of about 5.5 percent in 2014-2015.
Third, external risks have eased in emerging Asia, which compares favorably with some emerging economies in other regions.
Asia's emerging nations have moved rapidly to address vulnerabilities following last year's market turmoil and now have stronger macroeconomic bases.
So far, it has been good for Asia, but the outlook suggests that risk factors remain calm.
Unexpected restrictions on global liquidity will affect the region.
Domestic vulnerabilities can have even greater impacts, such as rising interest rates, vulnerabilities caused by the influence of large companies, and family debt.
In addition, the economy is weakening and has had the same impact as what happened a year ago when the market shifted to a change in future US monetary policy. .
Asia is facing risks from within the region.
Continuing to improve on structural reforms is important.
What does this regional vision mean for Cambodia?
If we have a long-term vision, growth in Cambodia is interesting but volatile in relation to its partners, in part reflecting its open economy and concentrated production base.
IMF research shows that external risks could confirm more than half of Cambodia's growth volatility, a trend expected to be significant as its integration with the global economy and with Asia is underway. Growing, in particular, is expanding the market.
Through trade, investment and financial networks, the economy is now more exposed to external risks.
Therefore, we need to be more careful about these risks.
The good news is that with the balance and consumption of Asia playing a major role in the region, including China, a new source of foreign direct investment and access to Cambodia's markets is opening up. Open.
In this context, regional integration in 2015 will also provide many opportunities for the country.
The policy challenge now is how to best tackle these regional trends with local reforms to improve the quality of growth and increase resilience against risks.
This is important to create better and more quality jobs for Cambodia's youth in the coming years.
In our latest report released in January and in the book:
The entry into a new phase of growth, released in February, shows the fiscal, monetary and financial measures that can lay the foundation for a stronger Cambodia.
Given the rapid growth of Cambodia's banking system and microfinance institutions, it is important to ensure that financial deepening can continue without compromising financial stability.
That would require streamlining medium-term credit growth and deepening financial management, better risk management and further oversight and regulation.
As monetary controls are limited due to higher dollarization, monetary policy will need the support of stronger monitors and prudent macroeconomic policies to mitigate increased risks in the system. This.
For fiscal measures, the implementation of government revenue collection strategies, debt management that may be associated with public-private partnerships, and the continuation of public financial management reforms are important to building Rehabilitation and protection of fiscal gaps.
The strong practice of tax collection in recent years is a good start, but it requires more effort.
Continued improvements in revenue collection and cost efficiency will support Cambodia's development needs, especially in health, education and infrastructure, and make growth more concentrated.
Asia's economic momentum is on an ongoing path where Cambodia is positioned to take advantage of regional trends and find ways to escape risks to create better and more jobs. For society as a whole.
Phnom Penh: The Polytechnic Institute of Cambodia (NPIC) in collaboration with carmaker Hyundai will open a car repair training center later this year.
The center was funded by Hyundai and construction began yesterday.
The center, located on the NPIC compound in Prey Popel village, Samrong Krom commune, Por Sen Chey district, was built at a total cost of $ 670,000 on an area of 360 square meters.
Construction is scheduled to be completed in the next six months.
Hoo Keun Kim, Hyundai Asia Director, who was present at the groundbreaking ceremony yesterday, said the purpose of pushing for the car repair center was to strengthen technical cooperation between Korea and Hyundai. And Hyundai Cambodia, and to meet the growing demand for vehicle repair technicians in Cambodia.
The project also received financial assistance from Korea Cooperation Agency Koica.
In addition to financial assistance, Hyundai will also provide Korean technicians to train trainees in technical skills and develop subjects that meet specific needs.
Students graduating from the center will be given the opportunity to do internships at the soon-to-be-operated Hyundai Garage in Cambodia, which has five branches and two Hyundai car assembly plants in Cambodia.
They will have a higher priority to get a job and become employees of the company.
Representatives of the Cambodian government have said that the shortage of skilled labor and the lack of skills training that responds to the actual labor market needs are priorities that the government must address as Cambodia prepares for integration. The local economy in the near future.
Pech Sophoan, secretary of state at the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, said Cambodia still lacked a skilled workforce for industry as demand increased.
Bun Phearin, director of the Polytechnic Institute of Cambodia, said the project would provide more opportunities for young people, including those who could not afford to continue their education, to become more proficient in car repair techniques.
Freight traffic through the two major ports continues to increase
Freight traffic through Cambodia's two main ports continued to grow in the first quarter of 2014 due to the good performance of the regional and local economies.
Shipments passing through the Phnom Penh PPAP and Sihanoukville Autonomous Port SAP increased by about 13% in the first quarter from a year earlier, the data showed.
Growth in Asia is projected to remain at 5.4 percent in 2014 and 5.5 percent in 2015, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) said on Tuesday.
Chea Sambath, director of SAP's Planning and Marketing Department, said the increase in exports was confirming Cambodia's steady growth despite the political stalemate.
He said that during this period, the port received a significant increase in exports of agricultural products, especially rice, and an increase in exports of garments and textiles.
Hep Bavy, director general of PPAP, said the increase in the first quarter was modest due to slowing regional and global economic growth.
Construction of refinery delayed until end of year
Cambodia's $ 2.3 billion refinery has been postponed until mid-November due to technical problems.
Han Kheang, co-chairman and representative of CPC, said the team was studying a detailed plan for the plant to submit to the relevant ministry for a building permit.
We are now preparing a detailed plan for the factory for the relevant ministries to decide on the construction.
I believe we can hit the ground running in mid-November.
He added that the refinery would be built on 80 hectares between Kampot and Sihanoukville and was expected to be completed by the end of 2016.
According to Han Kheang, the refinery will be able to refine 5 million tonnes a year.
The refinery has twice been postponed after the first construction was scheduled for early 2013 and the second in December of the same year due to lack of capital and time to complete an environmental impact study. .
Kim Natasa, executive director of the Cambodian Coalition for Resource Revenue Transparency, said the refinery would help prepare Cambodia when it was possible to pump oil, but that the environmental impact that the plant could cause must be overcome. Thorough evaluation.
Cambodian Senator Ly Yong Phat's disbursement of ANZ Bank financing to a controversial sugar plantation company has come under renewed criticism.
It is currently under investigation by Oxfam over four major Australian banks and their alleged human rights abuses in developing countries.
In the report entitled:
ANZ is meeting with families affected by the relocation of 8,343 hectares of land concessions in Kampong Speu province.
ANZ did not respond to specific questions regarding the Phnom Penh Sugar case yesterday, but acknowledged in a statement that it was reviewing how the company was handling its environmental and environmental obligations. Its society.
A statement from the bank in response to an Oxfam report said:
An audit report prepared by ANZ and leaked to the Post in January details Phnom Penh Sugar's failure to address health and safety concerns at the plantation.
Phnom Penh Sugar has come under further scrutiny by Australian media reports after two workers were killed by harvesters on the plantation six months ago.
Phnom Penh: The French Agency for Development (AFD) has provided more than $ 24 million in financing for water and electricity projects in Cambodia.
The loan is provided through the Foreign Trade Bank of Cambodia (FTB) to provide loans to the private sector, which supplies clean water and electricity to rural people.
The signing ceremony of the financing agreement between AFD and FTB took place yesterday at the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
Financing is divided into three types: long-term concessional loans, grants and payment guarantees.
He said that to solve the difficult problem of access to clean water and electricity for rural people requires the participation of key players, including the government, the private sector, direct practitioners and complementary financial assistance. AFD has been providing loans to eliminate barriers to obtaining bank financing for private entities.
FTB CEO Gian Vanit said at the signing ceremony that the funding would facilitate private entities providing rural water and electricity services with access to loans.
Un Yuthy, head of the Mining and Energy Working Group, said that in the past, commercial banks had not considered the potential of the water and electricity sector, saying that access to loans from other commercial banks was difficult. And high interest rates.
He said the loan for a private entity providing clean water and electricity was an important step in boosting economic activity in rural Cambodia.
Minister of Economy and Finance Aun Porn Moniroth said that AFD is an important development partner for Cambodia:
In an announcement on the EGA Stock Exchange and its website on April 23, the company claimed that it had been given 180 days until October 14 to increase its bid price from $ 0.87 to $ 1, the minimum price on the NASDAQ.
This announcement shows that:
Managers and chairpersons are evaluating these options to determine the best course of action.
However, if the company does not meet its target within the first favorable period and the second significant favorable period of 180 days, EGA shares are unlikely to be fully listed.
The $ 2.5 million company operates in Pailin province with about 26 gaming tables and 52 EGMs.
In January, the EGA announced it was withdrawing from its Pailin casino operation, which first opened in May 2012, citing a failure to attract cross-border gamblers from Thailand as the main reason.
EGA staff yesterday confirmed that the company was still monitoring operations in Pailin province, but declined to give details of the casino's difficulties.
So far, the attraction has not been shown.
He added that the lack of financial data from all 36 casinos located on the Cambodian border made it difficult to assess the real situation.
Cambodia to host international investment conference later this year
The Cambodian International Chamber of Commerce (IBC) confirmed yesterday that Cambodia will host an international investment conference later this year.
Prime Minister Hun Sen will open the two-day conference at the InterContinental Hotel in Phnom Penh, which begins on October 6.
The modern Cambodia theme of the opportunity that this conference seeks to attract international companies looking to invest in Cambodia.
In Channy, Chairman of the IBC Investment Conference Committee and Chief Executive Officer of ACLEDA Bank, said that the International Investment Conference provides investors with an opportunity to discuss Cambodia's economic vision and competitiveness with Cambodian industry leaders and officials. Government.
He stated in a press release:
The biennial conference, which in 2014 will focus on growth and exchange concerns with ASEAN integration, as well as Cambodia's young entrepreneurs.
Phnom Penh: Senior economist says $ 320 million is being lost annually as a result of the Cambodian people's confidence in the US dollar, while the long-standing lack of confidence in the riel means that the currency in A small number of districts are in circulation, limiting the NBC's ability to respond in a timely manner to the economic crisis.
He is urging the National Bank of Cambodia to consider establishing a new monetary institution that can control the riel interest rate and assist in the economic transformation of the Cambodian economy.
This is why I have asked Cambodia to consider setting up a Monetary Council as a temporary measure in its efforts towards democratization.
That will make Cambodia regain some of its lost revenue.
Cambodia is currently the largest dollar-consuming economy in Asia, with 85 percent.
But in 2010, when US dollar turnover was 80 per cent, the International Monetary Fund estimated that Cambodia's revenue loss was as high as 19 per cent of GDP.
Mey Kalyan, a senior adviser to the Supreme Economic Council of Cambodia, said large-scale revenue losses should prompt a shift in thinking from the National Bank of Cambodia, the NBC, the government and the Cambodian people.
He compared Cambodia's 7 per cent year-on-year economic growth and the inflow of foreign direct investment (FDI) due to the positive impact of the US dollar.
The confidence that the local currency has overcome over the decades.
After the abolition of all currencies by the Khmer Rouge in the 1970s, exchanges of gold, Thai baht and Vietnamese dong became common before the riel was re-established in 1980.
Two consecutive inflation crises occurred in 1993, when the value of daily living was seen rising by 100 percent, while the second inflation occurred in 2008, when food and fuel prices rose. An increase of 40 percent.
When people have confidence in the regulators and the ability of the government to control monetary policy, then people will be more willing to use the local currency.
The National Bank of Cambodia's ability to formulate a strong monetary policy is threatened by its reliance on the country's strong dollar with only 15 percent of its local currency.
According to the ADB and IMF, NBC's ability to act as a stimulus bank as a reserve bank for demand also faces increased risks as Cambodia's economy and banking sector continue to grow.
He added that such alarming risks were low.
Chea Serey, director general of the National Bank of Cambodia, said the bank was working on a draft regulator and a policy change to promote the denomination in Cambodia and promote the widespread use of the national currency, despite limited growth. Low though.
She added that a formal action plan for the US dollar devaluation, which would focus on improving the country's macroeconomic stability rather than establishing short-term monetary controls, is expected to be released soon.
She said the foreign exchange market was an interbank market where banks could trade foreign currencies among themselves, the securities market and the adequate regulatory framework in which they all operate is currently in short supply.
In Channy, CEO of ACLEDA Bank, said that by promoting Cambodia's agricultural sector, rice and rubber, the use of the riel is an improvement in the riel.
Aeon will attract tens of thousands of customers a day
Japan's Aeon Mall will officially open in Cambodia on June 30 after a year-and-a-half of construction with a number of well-known retailers, both local and international.
Currently, the largest supermarket in Cambodia has fully leased space to its business partners, with a total of 186 retail companies, including 42 local companies.
He told the Post after the meeting that the company had spent three years studying the market potential in Cambodia before deciding to invest $ 205 million in setting up Aeon Mall in Cambodia.
The opening of a standard level supermarket really fits the actual needs of the market.
He said Aeon Mall would attract about 10 million visitors a year, or about 27,000 a day.
Aeon Mall, located along Sothearos Street between the Russian Embassy in Cambodia and the Sofitel Hotel, covers a total area of 68,000 square meters, built on four floors and with a total floor area of 108,000 square meters.
The mall can provide employment opportunities for a total of 2,500 Cambodians, with parking for up to 1,400 cars and 1,600 motorcycles.
According to a list of companies that have leased locations at Aeon Mall, the Japanese mall will be a collection of internationally branded department stores, such as grocery stores, cafes, clothing stores, electronics stores and more. International entertainment and cinema.
The local brand has 42 stores, including banks, restaurants and clothing stores.
He claims that when the Japanese company can prove its success, they can help attract investors from other countries, such as Europe and the United States, to invest in Cambodia.
Hiroshi Suzaki, chief economist at the Business Research Institute for Cambodia, is optimistic about the launch of Aeon Mall, expecting to support the retail and commercial sectors in Cambodia.
Aeon has been operating a supermarket business with a total of 138 branches in many countries such as Japan, China, Thailand and Vietnam.
Challenges Expected to Reach 27,000 Customers a Day The upcoming opening of Aeon Mall along Sothearos Road has raised concerns about traffic congestion on the road. Strong tea.
Officials say specific plans to reduce traffic congestion in the area have yet to materialize.
City Hall spokesman Long Dimanche told the Post yesterday that there had been no discussion between Aeon Mall and city officials about a plan to reduce traffic congestion on Sothearos Road.
He said that in the past, there had been a meeting between the CEO and former Phnom Penh governor about concerns about traffic congestion in the area.
It is just a matter of concern that there has not been a formal meeting on the project recently.
Cambodia, Belarus boost economic ties
Belarusian government has agreed to provide technical assistance to Cambodia aimed at boosting economic ties between the two countries following the signing of a 23-day agreement and memorandum of understanding in Minsk, Belarus.
According to media reports on the 23rd, the agreement was reached during Prime Minister Hun Sen's first visit to the country on the 23rd.
Six agreements and three memoranda of understanding were signed in Minsk, including an intergovernmental agreement on the protection of mutual investment, an intergovernmental agreement on trade and economic cooperation, and The intergovernmental agreement on defense industry is a memorandum of understanding between the Central Bank of Belarus and Cambodia.
He expressed hope that the document, signed on April 23, would be particularly meaningful in improving the legal basis and treaty of bilateral cooperation.
In addition, FTA negotiations between the Belarusian Customs Federation and ASEAN are expected to contribute to closer ties between the two countries.
He noted that bilateral trade should be pursued through cooperation in finance, investment and innovation.
Prime Minister Hun Sen has called for more exchanges of delegations and business people to take advantage of the country's potential.
According to the government, in 2013, the total trade between Cambodia and Belarus was $ 6.3 million, almost double that of 2012.
Belarus' main exports to Cambodia are tractors and gypsum.
Belarusian tractors currently account for almost half of all tractors used in Cambodia.
The MTZ tractor assembly plant was launched in Cambodia at the end of March 2014.
