ASEAN to face influx of low-skilled migrant workers
Although ASEAN market integration is approaching, the flow of skilled migrant workers will be minimal when the ASEAN Economic Community (AEC) arrives in 2015.
Although much work needs to be done in the area of low-skilled migrant workers, which is not the focus of the AEC.
Pan Sosak, secretary of state at the Ministry of Commerce, said overtime, along with improvements in training and an increase in technical exchanges from Cambodian foreign companies, could provide more skilled staff in Cambodia. Area.
That figure excludes the large number of illegal migrant workers abroad, estimated in 2012 at 160,000 in Thailand alone.
With this, it is politically sensitive for low-skilled migrant workers not to be included in the AEC agenda, with development agencies saying ASEAN is slowing down.
As a result, migrant workers suffer as countries view it as a security risk.
In 2007, the ASEAN Declaration on the Protection and Promotion of the Rights of Migrant Workers was adopted by member countries, ensuring fair working conditions and protection from abuse in the ASEAN region.
But the challenge is that the ASEAN Framework Framework needs to be developed to strengthen the protection of that declaration.
The instrument has been in draft form for many years.
Yanavuth, executive director of Karam, said:
He said he wanted the instrument to be approved before the AEC was formed in 2015 and to be legally binding, requiring ASEAN members to amend their laws.
He hopes the draft law will take place at a meeting in Bangkok next month with the instrument agreed before the end of the year.
Cambodia's trade deficit widens to nearly $ 500 million
Official data from the Ministry of Commerce shows that Cambodia suffered a trade deficit of nearly half a billion US dollars in the first quarter of this year compared to the same period last year.
Experts say the deficit reflects the country's current growth in development, but warns it should be considered to diversify exports.
Data from the Ministry of Commerce obtained by the Post on Thursday showed that in the first three months, the country's total trade volume was $ 4.46 billion, an increase of about 15 percent of total exports. $ 99 billion, up 19 percent, while total imports reached $ 2.47 billion, up 12 percent from a $ 480 million trade deficit.
Mey Kalyan, an adviser to the Supreme National Economic Council, said on Wednesday that the current trade imbalance was caused by inflows of investment, official development assistance, ODA and rising domestic demand.
Hiroshi SUZUKI, chief economist at the Business Research Institute of Cambodia, said the deficit at this level was not a major issue for the current state of the Cambodian economy and that it was normal for developing countries to face trade deficits. .
Both experts warn that the country must try to diversify its exports and markets to prevent imbalances.
Mr. Mey Kalyan added:
If we let it continue, it will not be a good thing for us.
He said the United States and the European Union, Cambodia's main export destinations, were experiencing economic recovery, which would help Cambodia's exports.
Cambodia's main imports are raw materials, garments, oil and gas, construction materials, automobiles, food and beverages.
The main exports were garments, textiles and footwear, agricultural products, mostly rice.
Tongyang Securities cooperates with US brokerage firm
Phnom Penh: Cambodian securities firm Tong Yang Securities recently entered into a partnership with US brokerage Decker Co, the first US company to focus on Asian markets to provide US investors with investment opportunities in the stock market. Cambodia.
Han Kyung Tae, chief executive of Tongyang Securities, told the Post in an email yesterday that the partnership paved the way for US companies to invest in the Cambodian stock market, which was reached last month. Last December.
So far, there are dozens of American companies that have come to study and explore investment opportunities in Cambodia through Tong Yang Securities.
He said that through previous agreements between Decker and Tongyang Securities, the two companies had also co-organized seminars on investing in the Cambodian stock market in both Cambodia and the United States.
Decker has partnered with several brokerage firms in Asian countries and distributed information and investment opportunities in those stock exchanges to US investors.
Lumon Soly, deputy chief of marketing operations at the Cambodia Securities Exchange, said it was a good sign that Tongyang Securities had decided to partner with US companies, a mechanism that would allow US and Western investors to invest in the market. Cambodian shares through this securities company.
Ministry plans to stop importing used cars
The Minister of Industry and Handicrafts plans to ask the government to ban the import of used cars to Cambodia in order to reduce environmental pollution.
Cham Prasidh said that all countries must work together to reduce greenhouse gas emissions, which are thinning the ozone layer, and that Cambodia is beginning to gradually reduce its imports of environmentally friendly gases.
The new cars are no longer affected much, but the second-hand cars that are still in use are still flowing into Cambodia and still using environmentally harmful gases, so our measure is to ask the government to consider blocking the cars. Residents over the age of 10 are barred from entering Cambodia.
Seng Voeung, RMA's Ford managing director, welcomed the move, saying the government should pay attention to reducing congestion and accidents, emissions and also boosting markets. New cars are also on the rise.
According to the figures, tens of thousands of used cars are all imported to Cambodia.
It is often said that Cambodia is a dumping ground because we do not have import restrictions.
Some of the current used cars on the road are not qualified enough to cause accidents.
He added that closing the import of used cars would be able to attract car manufacturers to produce directly in Cambodia.
According to Seng Veng, used cars now account for 90 per cent of the Cambodian car market, while new cars account for only 10 per cent.
A used car dealer, who asked not to be named, told the Post he was happy to comply with the ban, but asked the government to reduce import duties to make it easier for consumers.
Kang Chandararot, president of the Institute for the Study of Development in Cambodia, said the move was too early for Cambodia as people's livelihoods were still limited.
He said old machinery and cars were boosting the Cambodian economy through agriculture and transportation services.
Even if the car is old.
If we have to use new machines, I believe it will cost a lot.
It affects those who use old cars to transport people and other goods. If it is banned, it will increase the cost and the transportation service will be higher.
Statistics from the Department of Transportation of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport show that from 1990 to 2013, more than 2 million vehicles were registered in Cambodia.
Artificial football table is popular
Prior to 2012, 6,000 square meters of land in front of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs was vacant and littered with rubbish.
But now the site has been transformed into a beautiful artificial turf football field and is home to about 500 people a day.
The venue is made up of six small football tables with a total area of 4,500 square meters, a cafeteria, a bathroom, a toilet and lighting for the night.
He confirmed to the Post yesterday that demand for the service was growing and that he expected to receive a total return on investment after three years, one and a half years. .
He said that football has become a multi-billion dollar industry in many developed countries and that business trends in this field are becoming stronger in Cambodia.
The construction of artificial football fields has steadily increased in Phnom Penh in recent years, and more and more young entrepreneurs are now focusing on taking advantage of the economic benefits of popular football. Establish a table rental business after seeing an increase in demand for this service.
There are no specific data on the number of artificial football pitches in Phnom Penh, but according to the observations of entrepreneurs in this industry, who similarly claim that artificial football venues are growing rapidly in Phnom Penh, and so far there are more than 10 pitches. Ongoing, which includes locations in Toul Kork district and the western part of the city.
Ming Hour, the owner of Ming Hour Home Services, has expanded his business by launching an artificial football table at the end of March 2014.
He has three small tables and is located in the center of the city, the former Chamroeun University of Technology.
The three combined tables have a total floor area of 1,606 square meters, with a total investment of $ 150,000, including a restaurant.
He confirmed to the Post yesterday that the new schedule always received a lot of pre-orders, and from 4pm to 9pm, it was full of customers.
Rental of tables for football matches varies from pitch to pitch and between weekdays and weekends.
When there are a lot of customers and need to turn on the lights to illuminate the rental price is higher than other times, and usually the busy time is between 4 pm to 10 pm.
At around 10am yesterday, a group of nearly 20 youths were kicking a friendly ball at the KB football table.
They are classmates and fourth-year students of Civil Engineering at Build Bright University.
Bou Udom said he and his classmates often came to play ball at the artificial table in their spare time because it was more comfortable and safer than playing on a regular field.
The creation of artificial ball tables often contributes to the creation of a number of businesses in the surrounding area, such as sporting goods stores and especially restaurants.
Kheang Puthy said the artificial football field could last up to 10 years and needed to be repaired every six months.
He plans to add the artificial football table in the near future, saying the demand for the service is still large, despite the current number of pitches.
An Asian Development Bank (ADB) analysis report released earlier this month found that Cambodia's tax department was found to be understaffed and understaffed, hampering its ability to collect taxes to boost revenue.
An analysis that considered the results of surveys from 22 tax revenue units across Asia between 2012 and 2013 found that the General Department of Taxation of Cambodia (GTD) is the lowest autonomous revenue institution in Cambodia. Asian countries.
ADB surveys show that GTDs under the control of the Ministry of Economy and Finance do not have the authority to set up their own structures, provide administrative funding, limit staffing or lay off staff, or negotiate staff salaries. No, while tax institutions in Thailand, Vietnam, Laos, the Philippines, Indonesia and Papua New Guinea are increasingly independent of government departments.
The ADB report also states that the inability to forcibly seize documents from taxpayers who receive search warrants without consulting government departments and other laws, such as banking laws that protect the confidentiality of customers, has been criticized. Impedes GTD's ability to access tax information.
GTD Human Resource Management is classified as one of the ASEAN components with the fewest changes, with no flexibility in staffing or ability to communicate in decision-making without professional initiative. Staff development does not have a system in place to manage rewards, incentives or periodic staff surveys in place.
Officials at the General Department of Taxation of Cambodia could not be reached.
