Visitors to tourist sites increase during New Year
Phnom Penh: National and international tourists visiting resorts in Cambodia's main provinces during the traditional New Year festival have increased significantly.
Chhey Chhorn, deputy director of the Siem Reap Provincial Department of Tourism, said that during the three days of the Lunar New Year, Siem Reap received 258,000 national and international tourists, up 44 per cent from the same period last year.
He said the increase in visitors was due to the three-day Angkor Sankranti event at Angkor Wat, Bayon Temple and Elephant Camp, which included concerts, traditional dance exhibitions and folk games. .
He said hotel and guesthouse rooms were fully booked and restaurants were busier than usual, although food and accommodation prices had risen slightly.
Minister of Tourism Thong Khon said at the closing ceremony of Angkor Sankranti that the cultural event had brought about $ 28 million into Siem Reap's economy.
Sihanoukville continues to maintain tourist growth.
Sok Seiha, an official with the Sihanoukville Provincial Department of Tourism, said the total number of visitors was 53,000, up 5 per cent from the same period last year.
He said clean beaches, adequate food and accommodation, as well as tourist destinations outside the beaches in the province were the reasons why visitors always wanted to visit Sihanoukville.
He said hotels and guesthouses were fully booked for three days and prices had risen slightly.
Som Chenda, director of the provincial tourism department, said the overwhelming influx of tourists in Siem Reap and Sihanoukville had pushed most visitors to Kep.
He added that better white sand beaches and roads leading to Kep were also factors in the increase in visitors.
Separately, Kampot province in this 3 days received a total of 74,000 tourists.
Soy Sinol, director of the provincial department, said that Phnom Bokor and Teuk Chhou resorts were still the most attractive of the province's 13 tourist attractions.
The Kampong Trach cave is attracting many international visitors.
Koh Kong province received about 12,000 guests.
Yuon Tean, deputy director of the provincial tourism department, says visitors often visit ecotourism communities.
He said the ecotourism communities included Tatai, Ji Phat, Bang Kayak and Trapeang Rong.
He said the province was improving the beauty of parks and beaches to attract more tourists.
