Only those who really like it and know the delicious taste of buying this prahok fish.
He has suspended production and is waiting for increased demand before he can consider restarting.
The Cambodian government in February added Siem Reap prahok to its GI list.
Kim Ly, deputy director of the Siem Reap provincial commerce department, said he was aware of the challenges facing prahok growers.
Siem Reap produces about 600 to 700 tons of leopard prahok annually.
The season for making leopard prahok is from December to March.
Ministry abolishes customs duty on rice exports
Rice exporters will no longer have to pay customs fees for rice shipments from May 1 after the Ministry of Economy and Finance decided to cancel them in order to strengthen their competitiveness. The industry.
The new decision has been hailed by Cambodian rice exporters and agro-industry experts as contributing to lower production costs and a new step in strengthening the country's competitiveness. Neighbors.
According to a press release from the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia, the Ministry of Economy and Finance has agreed to waive customs duties on rice exports in order to further enhance the efficiency of rice exports. Royal Government policy.
This declaration requires relevant officials to implement from May 1.
Kim Savuth, president of Khmer Food Exporters and chairman of the Coalition of Cambodian Rice Exporters, told the Post that the decision was a positive response from government institutions to facilitate exports following a request. From many rice exporters through a group of rice exporters in the past.
Many stakeholders in the industry, including mill owners, rice exporters and farmers, will benefit from the concession.
In the past, it took $ 15 per container to ship a container of rice from the factory to the port.
Thus, last year's cost of customs formalities for rice exports totaled $ 23.6 million.
According to Kim Savuth, administrative costs for transporting rice from the factory to the port cost $ 20 per tonne.
Independent agricultural expert Srey Chanthy welcomed the new decision, hoping to improve the business environment and make it more attractive to investors.
He said the government would lose some revenue, but the policy would benefit the industry.
New business registrations up 76% in Q1
Phnom Penh: Despite the political uncertainty that has hampered the business environment in some places, the number of new business registrations has risen sharply in the first half of this year, officials and the private sector say. Will be the same period last year.
According to officials, this growth is due to the government's deepening reforms and improved macroeconomic stability.
Data from the Ministry of Commerce shows that about 1,066 new businesses were registered as of March, up 76 percent from 606.
Foreign business registrations rose 66 percent from 294 to 488, while domestic business registrations rose 85 percent from 314 to 578.
Ken Ratha, a spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce, said that despite the political crisis, it did not deter foreign and domestic traders.
This increase indicates that Cambodia is doing well.
He said the increase in new businesses was due to a number of reforms within the ministry, such as the registration process, where some regulations had been changed to speed up registration.
