The World Bank has expressed concern that wage increases will affect some priority sectors.
The World Bank has voiced concern over the Cambodian government's $ 42 million increase in public sector wages, saying the budget could draw on necessary funds from education, health, roads and infrastructure.
The remarks were made at a press conference in Phnom Penh yesterday to release an annual report on the Cambodian economy, which takes place twice a year.
In 2014, the government's salary expenditure is estimated at $ 42 million, with all civil servants receiving a salary increase from 40,000 riel to 80,000 riel per month.
According to the World Bank, Cambodia has fewer government employees than any of its Asian neighbors, except for Papua New Guinea.
The average base salary of lower-level government employees in Cambodia is about $ 86 a month lower than in Laos, Myanmar and Thailand.
He cited the fact that public sector wages currently account for 5.5 per cent of Cambodia's gross domestic product and 36 per cent of government spending.
But if you spend the income from economic growth on salaries, it means you can not spend that income on education, health or continue to build roads.
However, he said the Cambodian government still needed more funds to facilitate a pay rise without having to cut spending in other areas.
Cambodia's General Department of Taxation collected nearly $ 192 million in tax revenue in the first quarter of 2014.
Council of Ministers spokesman Phay Siphan said the pay rise in the public sector would not affect funding in other areas such as education and health.
Asked how the government could potentially raise its revenue levels to fund the pay rise, Siphan said strengthening transparency in the tax system was a primary concern.
We will not raise taxes, but instead ensure that taxes are paid to the government in a transparent and accountable manner.
Phay Siphan added that public sector salaries were calculated based on current inflation figures and daily living expenses, including the price of rice and some basic food, as well as key salary measures.
