The event is organized by the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia and in collaboration with provincial authorities, Apsara Authority and relevant ministries.
Last year, the event was attended by about 3,000 facilitators, including artists, authorities and youth volunteers, and attracted about 170,000 national and international tourists.
According to statistics from the Siem Reap Provincial Department of Tourism, the number of hotels and guesthouses in the province, which is a major tourist destination in Cambodia, has about 166 hotels with a total of 10,382 rooms and 233 guesthouses with 3,600 rooms.
Ms. But Vanda, Head of Sales and Marketing at Angkor Hotel and Angkor Paradise Hotel, said that the organization of Angkor Sankranti during the New Year has greatly increased the number of tourists visiting Siem Reap and increased the demand for accommodation and restaurants. Significantly increased.
So far, the 169-room Angkor Hotel has been fully booked, leaving only the Angkor Paradise Hotel due to its slightly higher price.
However, Vanda said there was no price increase during the New Year, while room rates at the Angkor Hotel started at $ 35 per night.
She expects room rentals at Angkor Paradise Hotel to be full when the New Year arrives, between April 13 and 16, as bookings are on the rise and will intensify as the New Year approaches. To.
Ms. Pham Chhaporn, owner of Happy Guesthouse, which has 22 rooms, said that her guesthouse was fully booked for the New Year stay.
She raised the extra room rate by one dollar per room from the normal price.
If I have more rooms, I can rent them all.
This year's dry season rice yield fell by 1.9 per cent year-on-year due to a decrease in cultivated area.
This is according to a report from the Ministry of Agriculture on Friday.
According to the report, the declining land area was caused by the slow receding floods, which in some areas could not start dry season rice cultivation.
According to the report, Cambodia's total rice production in the dry season was 2.11 million tons, down 1.9 percent from 2.15 million tons last season, and the total cultivated area was 483,305 hectares. 2.5 percent from 495,465 hectares.
Experts say the small decline in crop yields was due to an increase in the average yield of farmers per hectare from 4.1 to 4.3 tonnes due to farmers' awareness of the use of varieties. Better cultivation techniques and increased water sources in some areas.
Mr. Pich Romna, Deputy Director of the Rice Department of the Ministry of Agriculture, said:
According to Mr. Pich Rom, the dry season rice varieties include Sen Pidor, Air 66 and Chulsa.
He added that dry season rice cultivation yields higher than wet season rice because dry season rice is a non-seasonal type of rice and does not need as much maintenance and water as wet season rice.
He said that the provinces with potential for dry rice cultivation include Prey Veng, Kampong Cham, Kandal, Takeo and other provinces near the Mekong River.
Nheb Sron, director of the Takeo provincial agriculture department, said that dry season rice production in Takeo was higher than last year.
He said the increase was due to the establishment of irrigation systems, which made dry season rice cultivation possible.
He improved the planting technique and added a water system near the farmers.
He added that major canals have been set up in four districts in Takeo province.
According to figures from CEDAC, there are 2,000 irrigation systems in Cambodia, including reservoirs, dams, canals and lakes.
Khim Sophanna, CEDAC project consultant, said that irrigation was still a challenge for Cambodian farmers and that access to adequate irrigation would help farmers increase their yields.
The Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia (SECC) and the Securities and Exchange Commission of Thailand (SECT) on Wednesday signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) on cooperation and information exchange.
The SEC is committed to working with all ASEAN partners in the Greater Mekong Subregion (GMS), especially to strengthen and develop capital markets through relevant counterparts around the region and to promote mutual understanding. On marketing and regulatory structure from one jurisdiction to another.
The Cambodia Securities Exchange has been re-established under the technical assistance of the Government of Korea through KOICA since 2007, and the Cambodia Securities Exchange (CSX), a joint venture with the Korean Stock Exchange, which holds a 45% stake, is now open to the public. There was only one state-owned enterprise registered in April 2012.
First quarter fishery exports up slightly
Total Cambodian fishery exports in the first three months increased slightly compared to the same period in 2013.
This is according to figures released by the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
Figures show that in the first quarter of this year, Cambodia exported 4,800 tonnes of fishery products, an increase of 6.66% compared to the same period in 2013.
Of that, 3,100 tons of fresh fishery exports and 1,700 tons of processed fishery products, including freshwater fisheries.
Officials and experts say the figures do not reflect the potential of fisheries resources across the country.
Sam Nou, a former deputy director of the Fisheries Administration, said the increase was small compared to the country's economy as a whole.
He did not specify the reason for the small increase in fishery exports.
Oum Savath, managing director of the Alliance for Fisheries Conservation Program, said the small increase could be due to an increase in fishing offenses and lax law enforcement.
He added that the number of fishery exports could be higher, which would bring revenue to Cambodia if the authorities strengthen enforcement of laws to curb fishing crimes.
He added that fishing offenses in Cambodia remain a problem, regardless of whether the government issues a restraining order.
Cambodian fisheries are exported to Australia, Vietnam, Thailand, China, Singapore, Malaysia and Hong Kong.
In early March, the European Union announced a ban on fish imports from Cambodia in response to the Cambodian government's negligence in addressing global crime.
Sam Neou said the vessel, which was fishing at sea, did not belong to Cambodia, even though it was flown with the Cambodian flag.
He said the answer could hurt the country's reputation as well as stifle traders seeking to export fishery products from Cambodia to the European Union.
Chhay Heang, a fisherman who exports to China, Singapore and Malaysia, says this year's catch has been modest.
He said his company exports between 7 and 10 tonnes of freshwater and marine fisheries each year.
Demand for groceries will increase in the New Year
Phnom Penh: Representatives of major consumer goods suppliers at the National Festival claim that sales of groceries, beverages and especially beer in April will increase compared to normal month sales due to Growing demand during the New Year.
Meanwhile, the general sales momentum during the Khmer New Year is unlikely to be as strong as in January, when many people celebrated the New Year. Traditionally Chinese-Vietnamese.
Hirami, president of a major importer and distributor of consumer goods and beverages in Phnom Penh, said yesterday that the general sales volume in early April had increased due to the Khmer New Year, but growth Not as strong as during Chinese New Year.
While sales in the Chinese New Year month rose about 50 percent from the monthly average, April sales were estimated at 20 to 30 percent.
This increase has mainly contributed to the sale of beer products.
He said that the supply of goods during the Chinese New Year is almost all kinds of goods, but for the Khmer New Year, the demand for goods increased significantly, only a few types of goods, and the demand for beer grew the most.
Khmer New Year is one of the biggest traditional festivals in Cambodia.
Civil servants, employees of private institutions, factory workers took time off from work and moved to their hometowns to meet relatives.
The move has also led to an increase in spending on equipment and products.
Consumption of food, beverage and beer products for gatherings is growing faster than usual.
According to a report from the Ministry of Commerce, Cambodia imported about 610,000 tonnes of foodstuffs last year, valued at about $ 241 million.
About 1,463 million liters of beverage products were imported worth $ 20.5 million, with a strong momentum during the beginning of the year.
Sov Sok Chamroeun, sales and marketing representative for Cambrew, which makes a wide range of beverages and beers, especially Angkor Beer, said demand for beer increased in April.
On average, beer sales in Phnom Penh are around 400,000 cases per month, and sales will reach 600,000 cases this April due to increased demand during the New Year.
Coastal tourism declines in Q3
Phnom Penh: Figures from provincial tourism officials in the coastal areas show that the trend of tourists visiting the coastal provinces in the first three months of this year has decreased slightly compared to the same period last year. 2013.
Experts say the downturn is due to a lack of improved transport infrastructure.
Figures from the Koh Kong Provincial Department of Tourism show that the total number of tourists in the first three months was 28,374, down 18 per cent from the same period last year.
Chhun Samkhit, deputy director of administration at the provincial tourism department, said the drop in visitors was due to the road conditions leading to the province, which was not as convenient as other tourist provinces.
Kampot province also showed a declining figure.
Soy Sinol, director of the Kampot provincial tourism department, said the drop in numbers was due to a drop in visitors to Bokor Mountain, one of Kampot's main tourist attractions.
In this regard, Ang Kim Eang, president of the Cambodia Travel Agency, said that the lack of infrastructure, such as good roads to major tourist areas, was a contributing factor in reducing the number of visitors.
He called for attention to be paid to building and renovating major roads to boost tourist arrivals.
While the number of tourists to Koh Kong and Kampot decreased, the number of tourists to Sihanoukville and Kep continued to grow.
Sihanoukville tourism officials said that the total number of tourists in the three months was 445,037, an increase of 26 percent compared to the same period last year.
Provincial tourism officials said:
The national guests come from better living conditions.
Either way, guests can now travel by land, sea and air.
Kep has seen a 20 percent increase in tourists.
Som Chenda, director of the provincial tourism department, said the increase was due to the development of infrastructure in the province and the promotion of the province through the Sea Festival at the end of 2013.
Guests also get to know Kep better.
He added that keeping guests safe also helped attract visitors.
Local brands start to gain traction
Green tea, milk and coffee are becoming very popular among Cambodians.
Those in the beverage and food industry say that local entrepreneurs should focus on building their brand to stand out in the market and gain recognition while competing with the beverage brand. But milk and coffee abroad are more abundant.
Mam Chanratanak, owner of NS2R Green Tea, says that the focus on brand building has made his shop as successful as it is today.
In just over three years, NS2R has expanded to eight locations and sold its brand to partners interested in his business model.
He said that the brand makes customers trust the product and also add value to the product.
Labels help customers recognize the quality of the product, and others can not be deceived by both quality and taste.
He said most entrepreneurs do not yet understand the importance of building their own brand, and that they run the risk of collapsing when a similar business is recognized by them. More and more internationals are entering the market.
Chan Si, general manager of food and beverage company Park Café, which has been in operation for 10 years, also said that building a brand requires time, capital and human resources, which most entrepreneurs overlook. But Park Café has been thinking about this since its inception in 2004.
He said businesses that did not pay attention to the brand tended to close down, as this indicated that managers of such business models did not have long-term plans for their business.
He can make money immediately using well-known brands.
Chi Seila, owner of TC Coffee Shop, the first modern coffee shop in Phnom Penh, confirmed that the TC brand reputation has enabled the coffee shop, which has been operating since 2003, to stand out in the market until now. While coffee is growing like a mushroom in the city.
We provide what customers need, so we do not worry about competition.
Regarding the entrepreneurial trend, Om Seng Bora, Executive Director of Karuna Project, said that building a brand now is a good starting point for Cambodian entrepreneurs.
Starting to think about selling our entrepreneurial brand is a starting point to prepare to compete with more international brands to enter the market during ASEAN economic integration.
He added that buying an accredited brand reduces the risk of starting a business.
He reminded local entrepreneurs:
A new report from the Asian Development Bank (ADB) shows that Cambodia's economic growth in 2014 will slow compared to the previous year due to the effects of the political crisis.
The same report shows that the annual inflation rate has increased.
At the same time, inflation will rise from an average of 3 percent in 2013 to 3.5 percent this year due to the effects of the tightening of tariffs imposed late last year on imports. The amount goes up.
But ADB senior economist Duong Poulang confirmed at a press conference at the ADB office in Phnom Penh yesterday that the rise in inflation was manageable and slowed from a year earlier. Has not had a serious negative impact on changing the living conditions of the Cambodian people.
Inflation, which has risen from 3 to 3.5 percent, although it is rising, is at a manageable rate and there is nothing to worry about.
According to the ADB report, this year's slowdown is due to prolonged political tensions since the July 2013 national elections and the movement to demand higher wages for garment workers. Disruption of some garment and footwear production by the end of 2013 will affect tourism, construction and real estate this year.
In 2014, industrial growth, which contributed significantly to garment and footwear exports to the United States and the European Union, was projected to slow from 8.5 percent last year to 8.7 percent. Percentage.
Growth in the services sector will slow to 7.1 percent from 8.4 percent.
If the weather is favorable, agriculture will be restored and may grow by 4.7 percent.
Hiroshi Suisaki, a senior economist at the Business Research Institute of Cambodia, told the Post that 7% economic growth was a good thing for the Cambodian people, even if it slowed slightly and would continue to contribute. Reduce the poverty rate by about 1% annually.
Inflation in Cambodia remained lower than neighboring countries such as Vietnam at 6.2 per cent, Myanmar at 6.6 per cent, Indonesia at 5.7 per cent and Laos at 5.5 per cent.
So far, he said, he has never had any reports that Japanese investment has been affected by the political situation.
He said Aeon Mall would open in June and Japanese companies, including shops and restaurants, would open as planned.
According to the ADB, Cambodia's economic growth in 2015 will be better than in 2014 due to the economic recovery in the European Union, the EU and the United States, which are both Cambodia's main export markets.
Cambodia's rice exports fall 11% in Q1
As Myanmar has more market access and more Thai rice imports into the world market earlier this year, Cambodia's rice exports in the first quarter fell 11 per cent.
According to the Myanmar Rice Federation in March, due to the new market exemption from import duties, Myanmar is at a turning point in rice trade with international markets.
In the past three or four years, the country has exported about 1 million tonnes of rice.
The new market will create demand for Myanmar's rice, which is expected to double by 2020.
The One-Stop-Shop Secretariat for the SOWS-REF rice export procedure shows that Cambodia exported about 8,433 tonnes of rice between January and the end of March this year, down from 95,228 tonnes in the same period last year. Last year, it was down 11 percent.
Kim Savuth, president of the Federation of Cambodian Rice Exporters and owner of Khmer Food, acknowledged the problem.
At the same time, he said his federation was not holding any meetings as all members were awaiting the formation of a new federation proposed by the government.
Ken Ratha, a spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce, said the ministry had set up a committee to set up a national rice federation to address the issue.
We know all these issues.
All their challenges are considered.
Mr. Kim Savuth said that in order to compete in the global market, Cambodia needs to promote the rice brand, especially fragrant rice, after winning the world's best rice award for two years in a row and diversify its transportation means to help lower prices.
Freight traffic through the two major ports continues to increase
Freight traffic data through Cambodia's two largest international ports shows that the volume of goods entering and leaving the first two months of this year continues to increase despite the country's unresolved political situation.
As the political stalemate continues to loom, the showing of growth in goods passing through key international ports is a positive sign that private sector representatives say are reflecting the normalization of domestic trade and general trade activity. .
Data from the Sihanoukville Autonomous Port shows that the cargo traffic in January and February through the port totaled 577,730 tonnes, up 19 per cent from 485,224 tonnes in the same period in 2013.
Data from the Phnom Penh Autonomous Port carried 18,415 TEUs of transit, up 20 percent from the first two months of 2013.
Chea Sambath, head of planning and statistics at the Sihanoukville Autonomous Port, said yesterday that the influx of goods at the beginning of this year continued to grow normally and the growth rate was slightly higher than last year, which could indicate From the stability of the domestic production chain and consumption of the Cambodian people.
The political situation is less affected because consumer demand remains stable and large.
The main goods transported through the two international ports are garments, footwear and agricultural products such as rice, cassava and corn, raw materials for industrial use, machinery, construction materials and other consumables.
Ieng Veng Sun, deputy director general of the Phnom Penh Autonomous Port, told the Post yesterday that demand for freight services in the first quarter of 2014 remained normal and that the effects of political discrepancies did not appear. There are still no serious negative effects on trade and commodity circulation.
Nguon Meng Tech, director general of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce, one of the largest private sector representatives in Cambodia, said trade and production activities in Cambodia continued to be normal and that traders did not appear to be too concerned about the current political situation. .
However, he cautioned that harmony between politicians was important, as this would help ensure greater confidence in the business community.
Data from the Ministry of Commerce shows that during the period from January to February 2014, the institution issued new licenses to a total of 708 local and foreign companies, an increase of 88% compared to the same period last year. There are 376 companies.
Officials Concerned Over Continued Decline in Cambodian Rubber Prices
Senior officials at the Ministry of Agriculture and rubber traders have expressed concern over the continued decline in rubber prices year after year as rubber plantations continue to rise.
Minister of Agriculture Ouk Rabun said at a conference on agriculture, forestry and fisheries on Friday that the development of rubber plantations was accelerating year on year in the 19 provinces.
Cambodia is not the only country facing this problem, so are the countries in the region.
He said countries in the region would meet to address the issue.
Of that, more than 78,000 hectares of rubber plantations with nearly 90,000 tons of dry rubber products for export are more than 32 percent higher than in 2012.
While the figures show an increase in rubber cultivation, the price of agro-industrial crops continues to fall.
Mr. Ouk Rabun continued:
He added that the continued decline in prices was hurting the incomes of rubber farmers as well as investors.
Regarding the drop in prices, Men Sopheak, deputy director general of the Chob rubber plantation, told the Post yesterday that the reason was that Cambodia exported half of the rubber and relied heavily on the Chinese market.
The price of natural rubber has continued to fall in recent years, with the average price of rubber now reaching an average of $ 1,980, a price that provides little profit for Cambodian farmers.
Considering the materials needed for rubber harvesting, the cost of labor is also higher.
In other words, the cost is more expensive.
Rubber prices fell as demand for Cambodian rubber in the Chinese market declined.
Men Sopheak says in the past his company has also tried to find export markets outside of China, but that other countries need less rubber than China.
He said that about 80 percent of Cambodia's gross domestic product was exported to China.
Recently, the Ministry of Agriculture released its annual report, which estimates that the area under rubber in Cambodia will increase to about 450,000 hectares by 2020, which will be able to provide about 40 to 500,000 tons of rubber per year.
Chanthy, an independent economist, says natural rubber prices will fall in the coming years if synthetic rubber prices fall.
He said that in order for Cambodia to maintain its rubber prices well, Cambodia needs to consider exporting directly to international markets, focusing on quality and low cost control.
It has to have a recycling base, storage base and transport infrastructure, logistics services, cheap transportation to participate in it.
We export to the market ourselves, which means that we have sufficient quantities to maintain and process stocks.
Once our product has a name, we can increase the price.
Cambodia's semi-finished rubber products are exported to China, a major market through Malaysia, Singapore and Vietnam, and a small number to the European Union.
At present, Cambodia has 60 rubber processing factories.
Hun Sen Urges Strengthening Partnership in Agriculture
Phnom Penh: Prime Minister Hun Sen proposed to strengthen partnerships between those in the agricultural sector in order to promote trade, trade and agricultural exports.
He was speaking at a conference on agriculture, forestry and fisheries held at the Chaktomuk Conference Hall on Friday.
He said that the support of farmers and the whole agricultural sector of the relevant ministries, national and international organizations, the private sector and the community is important to contribute to the closer ASEAN economic integration.
He also encouraged the promotion of agricultural production facilities, such as the use of modern farming equipment.
On May 10, 2013, the government passed the Farmers' Cooperative Law, which allows farmers to group and have the power to negotiate with traders about the price of their produce.
This law aims to promote agriculture.
Lim Bunheng, president of Loran, said that in the past, the relationship between his company and farmers had been good.
Yang Saing Koma, President of CEDAC, said that in recent years, Cambodian farmers have developed agricultural techniques to a good level.
He said that cooperation between farmers and traders is important to share information about the agricultural market, but to increase yields in line with market demand for adequate irrigation is the most important and important issue.
Cooperation between farmers and traders and technology is only one part.
The average annual rice yield is 3,163 tons per hectare.
Japan's AEON Microfinance Institution plans to expand its operations in Cambodia and diversify its products this year after two years of operation with good results.
So far, Aeon Microfinance has operated in Cambodia, focusing on installment loans for the purchase of motorcycles, home appliances, computers and telephones.
Dai Sukima Ida, CEO of Aeon Microfinance Institution Cambodia, confirmed to the Post last weekend that the Cambodian market was a booming market and that Aeon had received a total of 50,000 clients after launching in December 2011.
The interest rate for new loans in cash ranges from 2.7% to 2.9% per month and does not require customers to take collateral.
To date, the installment loan service from Aeon Microfinance Institution has been implemented in mobile phone shops, motorcycle shops, computers and electronics shops with a total of more than 1,000 shops, almost all locations in Phnom Penh and Siem Reap.
Vong Chhuong, owner of Lucky Motorcycle Shop, located on Sihanoukville Road near Preah Yukanthor High School, told the Post that the number of customers who buy motorcycles through Aeon installment plan has started to increase significantly in the last year. On average, about 30 customers a month apply for motorcycles through Aeon.
According to Chhuong, most of the motorbikes sold on installments are under $ 1,000, and most of those customers earn between $ 150 and $ 200 a month working in garment factories, hotels and restaurants. And other companies.
Nhim Tola, a 26-year-old mobile phone seller in Phnom Penh, came to apply for a motorbike on a down payment from a motorbike shop next to Lucky last weekend.
Giant Twin price survey phase, foreign investors show strong emotions
Grand Twins International will complete its public bidding session today after nearly two weeks of open bidding, attracting more foreign investors than local ones.
Sok Chamroeun, consulting director of Phnom Penh Securities Plc. (PPS), a securities underwriter of Grand Twins, told the Post yesterday that preliminary reports from securities agents show that investors are starting to show greater interest in the second week of the date. Book building from March 17 to 28, in which foreign investors such as Chinese, Japanese and Americans are more interested than local investors.
Grand Twin Book Building is done by 12 sales agents, all of which are licensed securities firms in Cambodia.
He said the reason why local investors were less interested was because locals were not yet fully aware of the stock market and because locals were likely to be concerned about production stability. The garment industry faces challenges related to the protests.
Foreign investors have a clear understanding of the sector and know that the company has a sufficient standard, they see the operation of a good company with high potential for revenue.
Representatives of Phnom Penh Securities have not yet been able to disclose the number of shares and share price offered by investors during the book building phase yesterday, saying it was a matter of principle.
The company has major markets in the European Union and the United States.
Grand Twin Financial reports show that the company has seen a steady increase in revenue since 2008 and a net profit of $ 8.78 million last year.
Svay Hay, CEO of ACLEDA Securities, said that the number of foreign investors interested in buying Grand Twins during the book building phase was significant, especially Japanese investors.
He said the investors had compared the operations of Grand Twin to major Japanese companies and other companies and saw that the company had many potentials.
The subscription phase, the second phase of the IPO, is scheduled for April 21-25.
Pursat: Following a series of international scandals, the construction of a 120-megawatt dam on the Atai River in Pursat province and the transmission line officially began yesterday.
Prime Minister Hun Sen presided over the inauguration.
The $ 255 million hydropower project, funded by the China Development Bank, has been in operation since June last year, with construction under 2008 under construction, operation and transfer of BOT, and a $ 113 million transmission line. Connecting the dam to Phnom Penh, Kampong Chhnang, Battambang and Pursat provinces has only recently been completed.
Speaking at the inauguration, Prime Minister Hun Sen praised the achievement and encouraged Chinese companies to continue investing.
During the five years of construction, the dam has caused catastrophic losses, including the loss of crocodiles, deforestation, the Cardamom Conservation Forest and the breaking of water pipes, injuring five people and killing four others.
But the Post found last month that illegal clearing in the area was continuing.
China is one of the largest investors in the development of hydropower projects in Cambodia.
According to the Ministry of Mines and Energy, more than $ 1.6 billion has been invested by Chinese companies in six dams with a total capacity of 928 megawatts.
Currently, five dams have been commissioned, and only one project, the 246-megawatt Tatai dam, is scheduled to open next year.
A recent World Bank report shows that currently only 26 percent of the population has access to the lowest rate of government-supplied electricity in the world.
Phnom Penh: A representative of the Ministry of Posts and Telecommunications of Cambodia said yesterday that Telecom Cambodia, a state-owned company, needed to restructure its business to resume operations as its competitiveness dwindled almost every year.
Presenting the Ministry of Posts and Telecommunications' 2013 performance yesterday, Secretary of State Sarak Khan said TC needed to carefully consider its current competitive position as key products TC has a smaller market share.
As of 2013, there were 42,535 landline subscribers from TC, accounting for 10% of the total usage, while Telecom Cambodia had only 5,446 internet subscribers.
He said that the provision of fixed telephone and internet services from companies is declining and difficult to recover.
