However, the telecoms project of Telecom Cambodia was postponed indefinitely by the government last year due to the company's unfavorable financial situation.
Chhun Sambath, director of the Securities and Exchange Commission of the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia (SECC), told the Post yesterday that the government had allowed TC to be listed on the stock exchange, but had not yet reached the stage where TC and the Securities and Exchange Commission Work together yet.
So there is no obligation to inform us.
The Post contacted Tong Yang Securities, the underwriter, by email yesterday but received no response.
Cambodia's fragrant rice potential is attracting Thai investors
Cambodia's fragrant rice potential is attracting Thai investors to open rice processing plants in Cambodia for export to international markets.
The quality of Cambodian fragrant rice is gaining more recognition in the international market after winning the World's Best Rice Award for two consecutive years.
According to the Bangkok Post, rice mills in central and northeastern Thailand are preparing to install rice mills in Cambodia, seeing the potential of Cambodia's fragrant rice production, where yields are rising and production costs are low.
The report said that limited experience in marketing and exporting of rice by Cambodian operators and the lack of advanced rice mills allowed Thai investors to expand their investments and would help boost rice export policies. Million tons in 2015 of Cambodia as well.
Experts and local businessmen welcome the presence of Thai investors in the industry with the expectation that the industry will take it to the next level.
However, concerns have been raised that small local rice mills could face stiff competition.
Lim Bunheng, director of Laurent Rice Exporters, said local companies at the forefront of rice exports were able to compete with Thai investors.
However, he said that there are still small rice mills that are still operating traditionally, making high production costs difficult to compete with.
For the private sector, if you are weak, you can be worried, but big business is not an issue, because the standard for exports can be the same as in Thailand.
Mr. Van David also said that the arrival of Thai rice mills would reduce rice outflows to neighboring countries and provide local employment while boosting the Cambodian economy. Well.
Cambodia will receive value added revenue.
Competition is inevitable.
In this regard, Srey Chanthy, an independent economist, said that the Cambodian rice industry still had room for further development.
Farmers benefit because when there is competition, farmers will get good rice prices.
He added that Thai investment would further boost the economy by creating jobs and pushing Cambodia's rice to become more international.
Cambodia's trade facilitation rankings much better
Phnom Penh: Although Cambodia's reforms have contributed to its rise in the ranking of trade facilitation processes, the country is still lagging behind the rest of the world, according to World Bank data. As a local partner.
Gerard McLinden, senior trade facilitator in the World Trade Organization at the World Bank, said improved customs procedures and improved port infrastructure had lifted Cambodia to 83rd in the index. World Bank's 2014 LPI delivery process.
Last year, Cambodia ranked 101st.
He was speaking as part of a presentation on the index at the World Bank in Phnom Penh yesterday.
The LPI ranking is based on a survey of more than 5,000 carriers working overseas.
The assessment relates to the country's customs management, shipping capacity and business hours.
Government officials from the Ministry of Commerce and the General Department of Customs and Excise say recent reforms are reducing bureaucracy.
But Srinivasa Madhur, director of research at the Resource Development Institute of Cambodia, said the World Bank's transport rankings for Cambodia over Laos and Myanmar were within the regional context that policymakers should focus on. Take care of it.
A survey of several companies operating locally in ASEAN countries found that Cambodia had the lowest reputation for improving the customs process.
Among the neighbors in the bloc, Cambodia has the biggest problem with informal payments, causing delays at the border.
Government officials vow to fight piracy
Government and law enforcement officials from Cambodia and Laos met in Phnom Penh this week for a training program on IPR law enforcement.
The meeting, co-organized by representatives from the US Department of State, the US Department of Homeland Security and the Office of Patents and Trademarks, is a three-day event in Phnom Penh that will focus on investigative techniques for identifying and prosecuting non-combatants. Respect IP intellectual property laws.
Minister of Commerce Sun Chanthol said that under existing law enforcement, Cambodia was stepping up its anti-piracy commitment with the current draft sub-decree to organize the work of all agencies working with compliance. Intellectual Property Law.
He added that a government task force had been set up to combat piracy.
