Taking rice stocks as collateral is still a problem for getting a loan
Taking stock of rice as collateral for loans remains a challenge for Cambodia's rice sector, as experts say the operation of rice mills has yet to meet the needs of financial institutions.
Lim Bunheng, CEO of Laurent and co-chair of the Private Sector Rice Working Group, told the Post yesterday that a lack of capital to buy stockpiled rice remained an obstacle for many local rice mills. .
So far, no bank has agreed to take rice as collateral.
He said the bank only accepts real estate as collateral and the appraisal of the property is low.
In relation to the loan issue, Mr. In Channy, CEO and CEO of ACLEDA Bank, said that his institution had previously practiced in accepting rice stocks as collateral.
However, he stated that the client must come with a financial report showing the financial status of the company for the last three years.
He explained that real estate, such as land, houses, buildings, etc., is the first important collateral to get a bank loan.
According to In Channy, in 2013, ACLEDA Bank provided loans to the agricultural sector of up to $ 323 million, equivalent to 19% of the bank's total loans of $ 1.7 billion.
However, he did not encourage millers to rely on rice as collateral.
He added that in the past, not many banks accepted rice as collateral because rice is a high-risk asset, such as fire, easy to transport from one place to another, and rice can lose weight if stored in Long warehouse.
Mr. In Channy said that in case the bank has to take all the rice in stock as collateral, all the rice will be sent to a separate warehouse which will be under the management of an independent institution that specializes and receives services. Rice and customer management costs are the cost.
He said that in the past, the obstacle was that local mills could not provide financial statements to the bank.
He said that this will help rice millers in Cambodia if each mill has a proper financial report.
He added that Cambodia still has many rice mills and rice exporters operating traditional businesses.
He called the receipt of rice as collateral theoretical, but to get financing from the mortgage, it requires an expert, a supervisor of the warehouse and the right legal standards. .
