Riel value remains stable against dollar
Although the business situation in Cambodia was somewhat affected at the end of 2013 after the election, the annual riel exchange rate against the US dollar remained stable.
The riel appreciated slightly yesterday against the January 2013 exchange rate.
The value of the riel last year fluctuated on average by 4 riel per US dollar.
The daily report of the Ministry of Commerce shows that the exchange rate of the riel against the dollar on January 1, 2013 was 1 dollar equal to 4,095 riel and on December 31 was 1 dollar equal to 4,115 riel.
Ms. So Phonnary, Executive Vice President, ACLEDA Bank, said that the exchange rate in Cambodia has remained stable since last year, as well as for the last 10 years, which is a good sign of an increase in credit. The public's mind is on the national currency.
According to the National Bank of Cambodia, the riel generally appreciated against the dollar at the beginning of the year and began to depreciate slightly in April after demand for the riel for the traditional Khmer New Year ended.
From May, the riel was stable until September.
At the end of the year, the value of the riel began to rise slightly due to the arrival of the harvest season, which increased the demand for riel spending.
Corruption in Cambodia Threatens Business
A report from the Leading Employers' Association of Cambodia shows that the corruption system has pushed trade to a safer level.
At a joint meeting with the International Labor Organization (ILO) and the Cambodian Federation of Employers CAMFEBA in Phnom Penh yesterday, employers' representatives called on the government to provide greater protection for those who say there is corruption and to investigate corruption in the Cambodian justice system.
He added that the ACU was the only organization with the authority to oversee these issues.
Minister of Labor and Vocational Training Ith Sam Heng declined to comment directly on the issue of the court's investigation, but welcomed the strengthening of the ACU's role in tackling corruption.
With protection, CAMFEBA set up its own mechanism last year to collect secret reports of corruption and bribery from traders, who then were able to collect reports and report to the ACU.
When contacted by telephone on Wednesday, Om Yentieng, head of the Anti-Corruption Unit, declined to comment.
