Cambodia helps find missing plane
Cambodian officials say the Cambodian Aviation Authority has increased its staff and is in constant contact with its Vietnamese counterpart in an effort to contribute to the search for Malaysia Airlines Flight 370.
MAS Flight 370, which was on the route from Kuala Lumpur to Beijing and has 239 passengers and crew still unaccounted for, went missing. From the radar system early Saturday morning in southern Vietnam, Koh Tral, Vietnam, Phu Quoc Island.
Chhun Sivorn, head of the Flight Orientation and Standards Division at the State Secretariat of Civil Aviation (SSCA), said the area was more than 300 kilometers south of Phnom Penh's air traffic control station, not far from outside Cambodian airspace.
He claimed that the SSCA had used emergency search radar and was working to conduct satellite surveillance with Vietnam after learning of the plane's disappearance on Saturday.
He said the SSCA had also increased its staff to monitor Cambodian airspace and was conducting analysis in the Gulf of Thailand to look for warning lights from air traffic control stations at Phnom Penh International Airport and on Preah Boulevard. Norodom.
The latest announcement from Malaysia Airlines posted on its website 48 hours after the plane went missing, thanks to search and rescue teams from Australia, China, Thailand, Indonesia, Singapore, Vietnam and the Philippines. And the United States, which is assisting in the search.
Although the research area borders Cambodia-Vietnam airspace, Sivan said Cambodia usually did not have the resources to participate in offshore efforts.
He added that large numbers of fishing boats and debris floating around the Gulf of Thailand could give false information to radar operators.
