Crocodile farming in early 2014 increased
Crocodile farming in the first two months of this year has increased compared to the same period last year.
According to the report, the number of crocodiles raised in the first two months of this year totaled 25,000, an increase of 12,000 or 48% compared to the same period last year.
Experts say the increase is due to the fact that most farmers now turn to crocodiles for skins instead of selling crocodiles to neighboring countries, while accurate statistics on the number of crocodiles on the farm are accurate. Than before.
Heng Sovannara, director of the crocodile development section of the General Department of Fisheries Administration, said:
Sen Rith, vice president of the Cambodian Crocodile Breeding Development Association in Siem Reap, said the demand for Cambodian crocodile skins was significant in the international market.
Our crocodile skin is very marketable.
Currently, traders from China are interested in importing crocodile meat from Cambodia.
The government has called on crocodile farmers to stop exporting crocodiles to neighboring countries since 2005.
Lack of capital and concerns about the market for crocodile skins remain obstacles for Cambodia to become a crocodile skin exporter.
Cambodia exports crocodiles, crocodile skins and crocodile meat to Vietnam, Thailand and China.
Farmers can sell a three-month-old crocodile for $ 12 to $ 14, while a three-year-old crocodile skin costs about $ 80.
Heng Sovannara calls on farmers to raise skins for re-export, as markets and prices are more stable.
Singapore alone needs 200,000 skins, as long as Cambodia has them.
What matters is the quality of our skin.
Farmers do not worry that there is no market at all.
Ung Visal, vice-president of the Siem Reap Angkor Crocodile Breeding Association, said the crocodiles on his farm were only one to two years old.
He expects to be able to export crocodile skin next year.
A report by the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries estimates that the number of crocodiles in Cambodia will reach 300,000 for export by the end of this year.
Another international port opens soon
The Cambodian government is studying the possibility of opening the Stung Bot International Gate in Poipet City to facilitate the entry and exit of large trucks, as the existing Poipet International Border Crossing is busier and more congested.
The new international gate is located on National Road 5 in Stung Bot village, Poipet commune, Banteay Meanchey province, about 4 km from the Poipet international gate.
The government decided on February 18 to set up a 12-member joint commission to open the Stung Bot International Border Crossing.
Kem Borey, director general of public works at the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and a member in charge of relations with the Thai side, told the Post yesterday that the study on the new international port project was to prepare for the connection. Better with ASEAN countries in the goal of easier trade when the regional economic integration comes.
He said it would take another two years for the new port to be completed and put into operation, at which point the old port would only be used as a gateway for tourists.
According to the 2012-2013 work summary report of the Ministry of Tourism of Cambodia, so far, Cambodia and Thailand have seven international gates, including a new gate, the Chob Koki checkpoint in Oddar Meanchey province.
Ly Utny, president of the Banteay Meanchey Chamber of Commerce and chairman of Ly Utny Import Export, said the government had been planning to build the Stung Bot International Gate for a long time, but had not yet made it clear. Grow.
Therefore, the establishment of a joint commission to study the current arrangements is a development that we hope the Stung Bat International Gate will have in the near future.
He said that the Poipet gate, which is currently in operation, is often congested by the chaos of large wheelchairs and gas trucks.
