Cashew nut prices still depend on neighboring countries
Kampong Thom: Farmers in Kampong Thom province are worried about market uncertainty and cashew prices as this year's harvest may increase.
Experts say market and price uncertainty is due to dependence on foreign markets.
Uncle Sovann, president of Sre Khmer, a cashew research organization, explained that the drop in both exports and cashew prices in 2013 was due to declining demand from Vietnam and Thailand, the main traders for the cashew market. Cambodia.
It is not until these two markets have odds that our export data increases because we depend on these two countries.
Thorn Ren, a cashew farmer in Kampong Svay district, Kampong Thom province, expects this season's cashew yield to be higher than last year, but says prices have fallen.
She added that natural cultivation makes it even harder for farmers to earn a living.
Chea Piseth, president of the Organic Cashew Association, said the price of cashews had risen slightly compared to a few years before the association was formed.
Currently, the association is trying to process cashews into finished products to supply the local market as well as export.
Processed cashew nuts are sold to the local market for $ 9 to $ 12 per kilogram.
Figures from the Kampong Thom provincial agriculture department show that the total area under cashew cultivation in the province as of 2013 was 23,400 hectares, with an average yield of between two and three tonnes per hectare, depending on the variety of cashew trees.
Unprocessed cashew nuts are exported to Vietnam and Thailand.
A report on the potential of cashew crops in Cambodia, released by the IFC in 2010, states that pre-export processing of cashew nuts adds up to $ 30 million to $ 40 million a year in value-added revenue, depending on production volume. At present.
