Joint aviation will be competitive
Experts say liberalizing the aviation environment for ASAM, the only aviation market in ASEAN, will lead to competition and price wars.
Representatives from 10 ASEAN member states met yesterday in Phnom Penh for a capacity building workshop focused on the EU integration experience for a single aviation market. Which came into force in 1990.
The deadline for the ASAM policy agreement is December 31 next year, coinciding with the opening of the ASEAN Economic Community (AEC) in 2015, which will see all member states united under one organization of the agreement. The joint trade agreement aims to facilitate the free flow of goods and services across the region.
Like the EU agreement, ASAM allows all ASEAN airline operators to operate non-stop flights, direct flights or stops between cities in the region under the umbrella of the Traffic Rights Agreement. .
But according to the provisions of the ASAM policy framework, unlike the EU system, all airlines must start and end travel in their countries.
Stephan Baertges, a representative for the European Aviation Safety Agency, said that after the opening of Europe's single aviation market in 1990, competition in the aviation sector had intensified, affecting some airlines.
Nine out of 10 ASEAN members have signed the liberalization agreement.
But Indonesia, which accounts for about 40 per cent of Asean's 604 million people, did not attend, and the issue is threatening to date 2015.
Aviation experts predict AirAsia, along with its Thai AirAsia and Indonesia AirAsia, could succeed and lead to a price war competing with low-cost carriers such as Lion Air.
According to Tan, Cambodia Angkor Air, the only Cambodian-owned company owned by local investors, is in a position to live in a market with a large number of airlines and still be able to compete in the Cambodian aviation market.
Keo Sivan, director general of the State Secretariat of Civil Aviation, said the regional aviation agreement would lead to an increase in tourism for Cambodia and provide growth for airlines owned by other local investors.
