In 2011, Thai Prime Minister Yingluck Shinawatra launched a project to buy rice from farmers at above-market prices, a project that is seen as controversial to the current global rice market.
Thailand is stockpiling more than 10 million tonnes of rice and is ready to release it after the rice stocks.
Thai jasmine rice now sells for $ 955 a tonne, down 17 percent from December last year.
The latest figures from Oryza show that Cambodia's jasmine rice was $ 885 a tonne at the end of February, down 7 percent from $ 950 a tonne in December last year.
Observers say the drop in the price of Cambodian jasmine rice is due to the supply of Thai jasmine rice to the market at low prices and large quantities in order to resolve the debt crisis that the Thai government has to repay to farmers.
Sales have declined in recent months, he said.
