Cheat Khemara said factories would face closure if production processes were disrupted as strikes continued.
Strikes demanding an increase in the minimum wage for garment workers erupted from September to the present.
As of November 2013, the total number of strikes was 131, a figure recorded by the Garment Manufacturers Association of Cambodia.
On Wednesday last week, Van Sou Ieng, president of the Garment Manufacturers Association of Cambodia, told local media that 80 per cent of factories would be closed and some would be relocated if workers continued to demand installments. Wages up to $ 160 are in response to plans by unions across the country on March 12.
Mr. Ieng called the demands unreasonable and could endanger Cambodia's textile sector.
He was referring to workers' $ 160 demands, which he said were beyond what employers could afford.
Van Sou Ieng claimed in early January 2014 that factories in Cambodia had lost about $ 200 million in the strike.
I have been resolving the crisis for workers for 10 years and since $ 45 I have not seen any factory leave Cambodia.
