Ministry of Commerce strengthens CamControl efficiency
Phnom Penh: The Ministry of Commerce of Cambodia recently announced the strengthening of the work of the Department of CamControl, an institution that controls the quality and safety of goods through the reduction of some complicated procedures to facilitate trade and accelerate. Export tracks.
The move was welcomed by exporters' representatives as the CNRP claimed that the Camcontrol department had overlapped with other institutions, and that its presence had made it difficult and time-consuming for traders.
Speaking at a luncheon with the EU Ambassador and a member of the EU Chamber of Commerce in Cambodia last Friday, Cambodian Minister of Commerce Sun Chanthol said he had decided to lift controls on exports. Check all containers that CamControl has carried out in the past, replacing only one of the total to make it easier for exporters.
Not only that, the Ministry of Commerce has also set up a hotline system so that exporters can report inactivity in CamControl, which can affect the export process of traders.
He said he would monitor reports of inactivity on the hotline and correct any shortcomings.
The Ministry of Commerce in this mandate seems to be working on a number of notable systems.
At the end of 2013, the ministry decided to stop exporters from applying for CO certificates of origin if they were exporting to countries that did not require CO, unlike in the past when ministry officials required companies to do so.
The Ministry of Commerce has also abolished the procedure, which requires exporters to apply for a one-year GSP certification once and for all.
Not only that, the ministry also seems to be trying to show transparency in its work than other ministries by improving the quality of information, while all important work activities are regularly posted on the ministry's website and Facebook.
Song Saran, president of Amru Rice Cambodia Exporters, welcomed recent developments at the Ministry of Commerce.
Because CamControl is responsible for inspecting the quality and safety of food, the organization's inspections of all export containers in the past have resulted in long exposures and unofficial costs for exporters.
CNRP lawmaker Son Chhay told the Post yesterday that the work of the CamControl department had been ineffective in the past, and that the body was set up to waste importers and exporters and disrupt their business operations. Many companies only.
Therefore, the past changes are just to facilitate the wrong things that the Ministry has done in the past.
He said the Ministry of Commerce should have general guarantees to facilitate the business environment, not just guarantees for large businesses, but to encourage small and medium-sized businesses by preventing threats from officials. To enterprise owners.
