Pepper is the vein of Kampot farmers
Kep: It is just one of many small pepper plantations of farmers in Damnak Chang Eur district in Kep province.
Nguon Preung is very proud of his efforts to achieve this half-hectare pepper plantation.
His family, like many others, have been growing Kampot pepper since the 1930s, during the French colonial era.
After being forced to stop growing during the Khmer Rouge era, many families returned to pepper cultivation in the 1980s and endured decades of low prices.
But today, thanks to GI certification in 2010 from the World Trade Organization, the situation is improving.
Pepper growers in the area are being rewarded for their struggles as newcomers are looking to capture market share here.
During this year's harvest, the total amount of pepper harvested is expected to reach 30 tonnes.
Kampot pepper is sold in four different types: black pepper, black, red and white.
Black pepper is made by drying green pepper, while white pepper is made by boiling red pepper.
Today, Kampot black pepper sells for $ 11 per kilogram, up from $ 4.5 in 2009 before GI certification was granted.
One stalk of pepper yields between 1 and 1.5 kg.
From January to May is the harvest season for nearly 200 low-income farmers in Kampot province.
But for farmers like Preung, planting has started slowly and the harvest may be in 2015 or earlier.
Not far from Mr. Preung's plot, about 15 kilometers away, was a Japanese company called the Japanese Investment Company, which invested in growing pepper on more than 60 hectares.
Moch Som, the manager of the pepper plantation, said that five reservoirs with 30,000 liters of water between the two plantations were used to irrigate 12,750 pepper plantations on an eight-hectare site. Out of their 60 hectares.
Through irrigation, plantation, a major industry boom, is growing on small plots of land.
Plans are underway for several more hectares.
Expansion is already planned, but no year has been confirmed to begin, so harvest numbers are difficult to predict.
Pepper plants can be pruned after 12 months for replanting.
As you can see, I am now preparing the ground for further planting, but we will have to think first.
Located in Damnak Chang Eur district in Kep province, another leading pepper plantation has been planted since September last year.
The plantation is a joint venture by four companies from Singapore, Malaysia, Hong Kong and China.
Chan Samen, general manager of the plantation, said cultivation was currently being carried out on 39 hectares.
The area is cultivated and covers 15 hectares with 36,322 pepper plants.
Mr. Samen said the province's harvest last year reached only 40 percent of demand.
Most exports are made through associations and to Europe.
Wanting to fill the current demand gap, Chan Samen said his company was looking for new markets in Asia, and especially in China, where they hoped global demand would grow even more. .
The price of cultivated land has risen from $ 3,100 before 2013 to $ 6,500 per hectare now due to the increase in the number of pepper plantations and there are no land disputes there as farmers get prices. By market.
