This year's rice exports may face some obstacles
Experts in the field say there have been some setbacks for Cambodia's rice sector this year due to the Thai government's contract to buy rice from farmers at a higher price than the current Thai market.
Thai Prime Minister Yingluck Shinawatra's policy of buying rice from farmers at above-market prices, which is due to end later this month, is upsetting the global rice market.
Thailand is stockpiling more than 10 million tonnes of rice and ready to launch after stockpiled rice collected from 2011, the year the policy was introduced, continued to grow exponentially.
Van David, deputy secretary general of the Cambodian Rice Producers and Exporters Coalition, said:
Regional exporters are examining whether Thailand will lower the price of its rice while stocks remain out of stock.
This factor can affect the global rice market.
According to a report from the US Department of Agriculture, the Thai government last month publicly auctioned about 150,000 tonnes of rice at a price 7 to 19 percent below market value.
Thailand still has tons of rice to sell in the market.
The rice harvest will end this month, but as of the end of January, the Thai government has already received 10.7 million tonnes of stockpiled rice.
This is according to a report from the US Department of Agriculture.
More rice in the market means more competition and lower prices.
The sudden drop in the price of Thai rice puts Cambodian rice producers and exporters in a very competitive position.
They try to sell the stock to 950, but actually sell cheaper.
Thus, our jasmine crashes and is difficult to sell.
