Cambodia looks at US as fragrant rice market
Phnom Penh: As Cambodian rice continues to grow in Europe and Asia, major rice exporters are also looking at opportunities to expand their fragrant rice market to the United States, expecting the country to become an important market. Next time.
The plan to increase exports to the world's number one economic power is part of an effort by exporters to diversify export markets to reduce risks while increasing Cambodia's rice production and supply capacity for international markets. There is more growth.
Speaking at a seminar on new market potential for Cambodian rice yesterday, Song Saran, CEO of Amru Rice Rice Exporters, said that many exporters would try to expand their exports to the US market. The country consumes a lot of fragrant rice.
Now they are using fragrant rice imported from Thailand and we know for sure that Cambodia's fragrant rice is really good quality.
The United States ranks 26th out of 66 countries to which Cambodia exported all rice last year.
Italy recently submitted a proposal to the European Union to consider tariffs on rice imports from Cambodia, arguing that the EU's preferential tariffs have had a negative impact on the rice industry of some countries. European countries.
In addition, in December 2013, rumors that Cambodian rice exported to Europe mixed with Vietnamese rice became a concern that could damage the reputation of the Cambodian rice industry.
To clear up these doubts, the Cambodian Ministry of Commerce yesterday also partnered with the Cambodian rice exporters community to formally publish a code of conduct to uphold the integrity of Cambodian rice.
However, repeated allegations of challenges affecting the decision to extend or suspend rice export preferences to the EU have been cited by experts as well as exporters. It is a warning to Cambodia that the EBA will not be immortal.
Therefore, Cambodia must be prepared to strengthen its competitiveness to maintain the growth of exports to Europe, even if there are no tariff preferences in the future.
Cambodia needs to find new markets to reduce risk.
Kim Savuth, president of the Coalition of Cambodian Rice Exporters, said that for 2014, Cambodian rice production increased with more new mills, and the United States was a priority market for Cambodia at the moment.
Administrative procedures to export to the US market are also easier than in China, Russia and other countries.
The important thing now is that we need to make buyers aware of Cambodian rice, they trust Cambodia.
We need to make sure we can afford to supply them on a long-term and regular contract.
Wing yesterday launched a prepaid Visa card service in collaboration with the Foreign Trade Bank of Cambodia (FTB) after a six-month delay.
FTB is now the second largest bank to offer financial products through mobile payments and money transfer companies.
Wing, a non-bank, is not allowed to issue its own Visa card without the cooperation of financial institutions, which are currently licensed to operate third parties to provide local remittance products through ANZ Bank.
FTB Wing Visa Card was set to launch in August last year, but the issuance of a license by the bank's regulator has delayed the process.
The new card can be used abroad wherever Visa is issued and can be topped up using an existing Wing Mobile Money account or by sending instant cash at WING Deposits and Deposits nationwide.
Ministry releases rice ethics
The European Union is one of Cambodia's largest rice markets.
The code of conduct comes after concerns that Cambodia could lose all its benefits except for the EBA, which has access to its largest market.
The EBA agreement with the EU allows Cambodia to have an advantage over neighboring countries such as Vietnam and Thailand in exporting rice to European countries, regardless of quotas and import tariffs.
Exporting rice from Cambodia that does not originate from Cambodia is in violation of this agreement.
The press release states that the Code aims to ensure the consistent performance of all Cambodian rice exporters regarding the origins of Cambodian rice exports and to ensure the commitment of the Cambodian rice exporters community in safeguarding favorable conditions. Trade is within the EBA framework.
Kim Savuth, president of the Federation of Cambodian Rice Exporters and a signatory to the code, said this was in response to EU accusations against Cambodian rice exporters mixed with Vietnamese rice.
We really sell Cambodian goods.
He added that this code of conduct will help the reputation of Cambodia's rice, the reputation of Cambodian rice exporters and the reputation of Cambodia.
Brokers, rice collectors, millers and exporters are encouraged to sign the code.
Rice exporters applying for a certificate of origin from the Ministry of Commerce will be required to provide proof of receipt and receipt that the rice originated in Cambodia.
The Audit Committee, which includes representatives from both industries and non-industrial organizations, is tasked with investigating allegations of fraud by EU customs.
Fines can be as severe as a permanent suspension of a rice exporter from obtaining a certificate of origin and the right to import rice into the European market.
Exports to the EU accounted for about 60 percent of total rice exports, with France, Poland and the Netherlands among the top export destinations.
Ministry announces selection of tobacco leaf exporters
Phnom Penh: The Ministry of Commerce of Cambodia last week announced the selection of tobacco leaf manufacturers to apply for public bidding for the right to export tobacco leaves to Vietnam in 2014.
This is the first time the ministry has put out a public bid.
Vietnam has been one of Cambodia's main tobacco markets in the past as the country has offered quotas to Cambodia.
Quotas are a preferential tariff system that allows Cambodia to import tobacco leaves to Vietnam without paying customs duties, but with an agreed amount.
Every year, Cambodia receives a quota to export 3,000 tons of tobacco leaves to Vietnam.
According to the announcement of the Ministry of Commerce, all local and foreign companies that are properly registered in Cambodia can participate in public bidding to be able to export tobacco leaves to Vietnam in 2014.
The announcement was made by the Tobacco Quota Bidding Commission on Wednesday, February 12.
Ou Sophal, director of the accounting department at the Ministry of Commerce, said that for 2014, the Ministry of Commerce had put up a quota for tobacco exports provided by Vietnam to Cambodia, hoping to provide more state revenue.
The bidding is very accurate, each company can participate in the bidding equally.
He said that accurate bidding could encourage participating companies to pay higher prices for export rights, which would contribute to increasing state revenue from these exports.
The closing date for applications is February 18, and the auction will take place at the Ministry of Commerce on February 19.
Most tobacco crops are grown by Cambodians in riverine provinces such as Kampong Cham, Kandal and Kratie.
According to the Ministry of Commerce, Kampong Cham is the largest producer of tobacco, with an area of more than 8,000 hectares and a total annual yield of 13,116 tons.
Sim Thavirak, director of the Kampong Cham Provincial Department of Agriculture, told the Post yesterday that the area under tobacco cultivation in Kampong Cham seems to have declined slightly in recent years as farmers turned to other crops, such as corn, beans and sesame. Original instead.
But he said tobacco was still important to farmers living along the river, which helped provide them with an additional source of income.
Kuy Huot, director of the Kratie Provincial Department of Agriculture, similarly mentioned the decline in tobacco cultivation in Kratie in recent years as farmers turned to other agro-industrial crops instead.
The area under tobacco cultivation in Kratie province is around 800 hectares per year.
